## The idea

Count each value. A count is between 1 and n, so make an array of **buckets indexed by count** and drop each value into its bucket. Then read buckets from the highest count down until you have k values.

## Step by step

1. Count with a dictionary.
2. buckets[count] = list of values with that count.
3. Walk counts from n down to 1; sort each bucket (tie rule: smaller first); take values until k.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [1,1,1,2,2,3], k = 2

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| counts | 1→3, 2→2, 3→1 |
| buckets | [3] = {1}, [2] = {2}, [1] = {3} |
| read from 6 down | take 1, then 2 → done |

**Result:** [1, 2]

## Cost

O(n) time and memory (bucket sort, no comparison sort over all values).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

